This PR implements the Collection pattern to Autocomplete, this implements the OOTB features of the Collection such as list virtualization (enabled by default), static content and dynamic content.

Dynamic content has a filter by default as does static content. The filter can be customized when `items` is behaved as controlled, for example this is good for cases where the filter is done by an asynchronous request instead of the filter happening in the browser with all the data.

```jsx
<ClayAutocomplete
  defaultItems={['one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five']}
  messages={{
    loading: 'Loading...',
    notFound: 'No results found',
  }}
>
  {(item) => (
    <ClayAutocomplete.Item key={item}>
      {item}
    </ClayAutocomplete.Item>
  )}
</ClayAutocomplete>
```

In the example above the filter is enabled because the filter is done directly in the browser instead of an asynchronous request. The developer just passes the items to the `defaultItems` property which has the behavior of uncontrolled. When the filter is done via request the component must be controlled.

```tsx
const [value, setValue] = useState('');

const [networkStatus, setNetworkStatus] = useState<NetworkStatus>(
  NetworkStatus.Unused
);

const {resource} = useResource({
  fetchPolicy: FetchPolicy.CacheFirst,
  link: 'https://rickandmortyapi.com/api/character/',
  onNetworkStatusChange: setNetworkStatus,
  variables: {name: value},
});

<ClayAutocomplete
  loadingState={networkStatus}
  items={resource?.results ?? []}
  messages={{
    loading: 'Loading...',
    notFound: 'No results found',
  }}
  onChange={setValue}
  onItemsChange={() => {}}
  placeholder="Enter a name"
  value={value}
>
  {(item) => (
    <ClayAutocomplete.Item key={item.id}>
      {item.name}
    </ClayAutocomplete.Item>
  )}
</ClayAutocomplete>
```

